# Git attributes — canonical checkout/commit line endings and binary handling.
# Owner: Brett (architecture / cross-cutting policy). Ruled and written by Brett at Tyler's proposal.
#
# There is no git repository in this tree today (checked 2026-10-30). This file is written now
# for two reasons: it documents the policy beside .editorconfig, and it makes the policy
# enforceable by construction the moment a repository exists, instead of relying on memory.
#
# WHY: supplied-models.ts oscillated between 5608 B CRLF (1d7c57a7...) and 5553 B LF
# (1667ee4a...) with identical content, because two runtimes wrote two conventions and nothing
# canonicalised them. See .editorconfig for the full note and its stated limit.
#
# The limit applies here too: git attributes govern GIT's checkout/commit filters, not arbitrary
# scripted writes. A Python `open(path,'w')` on Windows still emits CRLF. Scripted writers must
# pass newline='\n' (Python) or write LF-normalised text (Node).

* text=auto eol=lf

# Text artefacts that are cited by hash: LF only, so a digest identifies content and not encoding.
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.tsx  text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.scss text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Binary artefacts: never diffed, never normalised, never line-ending converted.
*.glb  binary
*.gltf binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ktx2 binary
*.basis binary
*.wasm binary
*.zip  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
